#: ../../source/docs/startstop-switch.rst:5
#: 1e004060d76d40dbb92dfb129ef0210e
msgid "Start/Stop Switch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/startstop-switch.rst:7
#: 12125f2b3edf41a5bd029a9770870e17
msgid "For some robotics competitions, RC control is not allowed and the rover must be started in :ref:`Auto <auto-mode>` mode with an onboard switch. This article explains how to set up such a switch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/startstop-switch.rst:16
#: 4eec2ba0a8e04d1580c3bbcf791744d4
msgid "You don't necessarily need to use a switch for this. If you enable the :ref:`AUTO_KICKSTART <AUTO_KICKSTART>` function you can just kick your rover [not too hard!] and the sensed acceleration will start the vehicle automatically. But if you don't want to kick your car, a switch is the way to go)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/startstop-switch.rst:22
#: f10f410785584c4dad81de282c821c13
msgid "Physical wiring"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/startstop-switch.rst:24
#: 060e4a27363344c49a5bc8d7dffadf26
msgid "The way this function works is that an APM port is assigned to the Start/Stop switch and APM will wait for that pin to be brought low (connected to ground). When this function is enabled, the Rover code will not send power to the rover's motor in Auto mode until that pin state is detected. So the rover will just stay stationary, waiting for you to flick the switch. Once you do so, it will perform its Auto mission as usual. You can stop the motion at any time by turning the switch back to the off position"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/startstop-switch.rst:33
#: 770c5794d84c4754b905d59cc2c27431
msgid "Any two-position switch will work, from a toggle (like `this one <https://www.sparkfun.com/products/9276>`__) to a slider as shown. There should only be two terminals. Wire one to the white wire of a RC servo cable and the other to the black wire as shown below. This way, when the switch is in the \"on\" position it connects the ground and signal pins."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/startstop-switch.rst:42
#: bc2edd32ceb945d3b3cec2927b4153ee
msgid "Plug that connector into any spare APM port, from A0-A8 (the ports above 8 are not digital pins and cannot be used). We typically use A4, since A0-A3 are used by the sonar sensors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/startstop-switch.rst:47
#: c09ccd4100de41499c13882cd91738dd
msgid "Software setup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/startstop-switch.rst:49
#: 54da5e0c38564adfaf114ec9c69e07bb
msgid "You can enable this switch in the Advanced Parameters List by entering the number of the APM port you've connected the switch to in the :ref:`AUTO_TRIGGER_PIN <AUTO_TRIGGER_PIN>` parameter. In the example below, we've entered 4 because the switch is plugged into port A4."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/startstop-switch.rst:58
#: fa909d54064c450f913bba499af62cf4
msgid "If you're not using RC at all and can't switch modes manually, you may want to set your APM so that it boots into Auto mode at power-up (the auto-start function only works in Auto mode). You can do that by setting :ref:`INITIAL_MODE <INITIAL_MODE>` to 10 (see parameter for other options)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/startstop-switch.rst:67
#: 290988398acf4571957c69687351bec5
msgid "Once this is set up, all you need to do at robotics competitions is power on your rover, wait for GPS lock and when you get the green light, flick the switch. The rover should take it from there!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/startstop-switch.rst:73
#: 144cc9af73a0491cb586fc032fc5a910
msgid "Depending on your failsafe settings, the rover may not move if you don't have GPS lock. Check for that before flicking the Auto Start switch)"
msgstr ""
